1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,897
This checkout looks done.

2
00:00:01,957 --> 00:00:04,246
It isn't — and nobody checked.

3
00:00:04,306 --> 00:00:06,438
Watch: Pay is clicked twice.

4
00:00:06,498 --> 00:00:07,455
One checkout.

5
00:00:07,515 --> 00:00:09,412
Two orders in the ledger.

6
00:00:09,472 --> 00:00:11,213
Each stock down by two.

7
00:00:11,273 --> 00:00:15,832
The total on screen still says forty-five
pounds — correct.

8
00:00:15,892 --> 00:00:18,240
The money underneath is wrong.

9
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:23,342
Kane Loop is a watcher that blocks on a
diff, not on a claim.

10
00:00:23,402 --> 00:00:25,176
One command installs it.

11
00:00:25,236 --> 00:00:27,086
One save starts the gate.

12
00:00:27,146 --> 00:00:36,944
It watches the repo: a save runs the gate,
Kane checks the checkout, and a red
verdict blocks the ship until the fix is
verified.

13
00:00:37,004 --> 00:00:39,908
No dashboard, no 'trust me, it works.'

14
00:00:40,408 --> 00:00:43,711
That double charge is the seeded bug —
disclosed up front.

15
00:00:43,771 --> 00:00:49,163
LoopMart is a checkout with three
products, one discount code, and one bug
planted on purpose.

16
00:00:49,223 --> 00:00:56,933
But the first live run caught a real one
first: Pay stuck on Processing, a timer
detached from the window, so the payment
never fired.

17
00:00:56,993 --> 00:01:02,037
The checkout was completely broken in a
real browser — and the unit tests had
missed it.

18
00:01:02,097 --> 00:01:03,660
Result code seven-four-zero.

19
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:06,040
One fix later, the core flow went green.

20
00:01:06,540 --> 00:01:08,281
The loop itself runs on real output.

21
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:12,284
One hundred tests pass — parser,
classifier, the checkout, the fixer, the
sweep.

22
00:01:12,344 --> 00:01:17,087
Sixteen live runs, eight hundred twenty
credits spent — every number on screen is
a real result.

23
00:01:17,147 --> 00:01:21,641
Then Kane ran the checkout live, and the
ledger check failed: the same order
charged twice.

24
00:01:21,701 --> 00:01:25,693
Result code seven-four-zero — the
classifier's verdict, not a claim from the
app.

25
00:01:25,753 --> 00:01:32,508
The gate needs two greens before it calls
anything verified: the same test replayed,
and a fresh sweep that works the total out
itself.

26
00:01:33,008 --> 00:01:41,222
The fix is one patch, twenty-eight lines
across two files — a single-flight guard
and an idempotency guard.

27
00:01:41,282 --> 00:01:47,640
The loop applies its own bounded patch for
the bug class: one patch, no test edits.

28
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,892
Toggle the mode and the same double-click
charges once.

29
00:01:51,952 --> 00:01:54,968
What you are seeing is the fixed build.

30
00:01:55,468 --> 00:01:56,629
So what happened?

31
00:01:56,689 --> 00:01:58,568
Kane ran the checkout live.

32
00:01:58,628 --> 00:02:05,607
The core flow passed, and the chaos sweep
went green on retry: one order, result
code one hundred.

33
00:02:05,667 --> 00:02:10,850
Same test, same checkout — and Kane
checked it a second, independent way.

34
00:02:10,910 --> 00:02:15,806
One hundred tests, sixteen live runs, two
independent green verdicts.

35
00:02:15,866 --> 00:02:19,828
And the ledger now survives a new order —
verified live.

36
00:02:19,888 --> 00:02:24,556
Replay unproven — Kane's analyzer failed
four times, not the app.

37
00:02:25,056 --> 00:02:31,262
Kane Loop: a watcher any agent can install
in one command, that blocks on a diff, not
on a claim.

38
00:02:31,322 --> 00:02:33,265
Try it — one link, one command.

39
00:02:33,325 --> 00:02:34,488
Break it yourself.
